{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"dd10b3170045a7d93acb33765a6e44567e3833cefbbb0cf69d590dc4919e700f","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_1406e4f6d344e59dd3436f299fa8629bff2f7aaa786e267fa3976376cb927f8e","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a technically sound framework for removing inter-experimental variability in systems neuroscience, grounded in a clear theoretical formulation of domain adaptation. The experimental design is robust, utilizing a large-scale dataset with anatomical ground truth for validation, and the authors demonstrate a strong trade-off between signal preservation and domain invariance. Reproducibility is supported by detailed descriptions of the training process and a commitment to open-sourcing the code. The statistical analysis is appropriate for the data, employing multiple seeds and non-parametric tests where necessary.","paper_id":"lVmIjQiJJSr","paper_identity_sha256":"f9b1c4a64632829711d53429ec7128b36524187aaf6b63501893f305bb296fbe","prompt_identity_sha256":"5a8036589a2118ba7fc70f799c60618efda98f48c85e0b641096523c7154159a","raw_response_sha256":"f3eb6d4f29b4356918bf2b11bc467ea829e9c67c44b9d3b7a6b7ac3e5bf797c0","request_identity_sha256":"0964874e62f9b1747994f8980624656b088b30d1c1ad40ad5d90431dc1e7923b","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"acc674ae1538ba3be68faf4b169292ff81cde1149e4513857d9559277cb1cfe9","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-008","SRC-009","SRC-010","SRC-011","SRC-012"],"review_text":"The methodological framework is well-formulated, casting the removal of inter-experimental variability as a domain adaptation problem. The authors define a clear objective function based on mutual information, distinguishing between supervised and unsupervised settings. The transition from the theoretical mutual information objective to a practical adversarial optimization scheme is logically consistent, utilizing a min-max game to minimize domain predictability while maximizing signal reconstruction. The use of a variational lower bound for mutual information estimation is a standard and appropriate technique for this type of problem. No major conceptual or implementation flaws are apparent in the derivation of the loss functions or the optimization strategy.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-MET"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-013","SRC-014","SRC-019","SRC-020","SRC-023","SRC-028"],"review_text":"The experimental design is comprehensive, utilizing a large-scale dataset of two-photon imaging recordings from retinal bipolar cells. The choice of metrics is appropriate for the task, including the Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) for dataset mixing and cell type classification accuracy. A significant strength is the validation of the unsupervised and semi-supervised predictions against anatomical cell type distributions derived from electron microscopy data, which provides an independent ground truth for the biological signal. The downstream analysis demonstrating the unmasking of a known biological effect (decorrelation of responses) further validates the utility of the method. The comparison between raw data and corrected data clearly illustrates the impact of inter-experimental variability.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-015","SRC-016","SRC-034"],"review_text":"The paper provides sufficient detail for reproduction, describing the random splitting of data into training, validation, and test sets, and the use of random search for hyperparameter optimization. The authors explicitly state that the training setup, model architectures, data processing, and random search details are fully described in the Appendix. Furthermore, the authors commit to making the code toolbox available as open source upon publication. While the specific hyperparameter values are deferred to the Appendix, the transparency of the training protocol and the promise of code availability support reproducibility.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-REP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-007","SRC-017","SRC-020"],"review_text":"The paper compares the proposed method (RAVE and RAVE+) against relevant prior work, including a linear baseline and two state-of-the-art methods from single-cell genomics (Harmony and scGen). The selection of baselines is justified by their performance in benchmarking studies for batch effect correction. The comparison is fair, as all methods are evaluated on the same dataset and using the same evaluation metrics. The results clearly show that the proposed method achieves a better trade-off between removing inter-experimental variability and preserving biological signal compared to the baselines. The related work section adequately positions the contribution within the context of domain adaptation and batch effect correction.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-CMP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-020","SRC-025","SRC-028"],"review_text":"The statistical rigor is appropriate for the experimental setup. The authors report standard deviations across 10 seeds for the classifier, providing a measure of variance in the results. In the downstream analysis, a non-parametric paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test is used to assess the significance of the change in correlation between local and full-field responses, with a reported p-value of less than 0.0001. This indicates that the observed effects are statistically meaningful. The use of multiple seeds and appropriate statistical tests for the specific type of data (paired comparisons) demonstrates careful evaluation of the results.","subdimension_id":"QUAL-STA"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"81b15beb9176271a7395c539d27ebec7ca281bfe3a173786f2e1c7e8ddf49920","paper_id":"lVmIjQiJJSr","paper_ordinal":115,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"f3eb6d4f29b4356918bf2b11bc467ea829e9c67c44b9d3b7a6b7ac3e5bf797c0","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"lVmIjQiJJSr","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"ec08cea2af19a94fd86a0f4523b2fee72eabb202daf1100b8a74a54a5ed0f271","request_identity_sha256":"0964874e62f9b1747994f8980624656b088b30d1c1ad40ad5d90431dc1e7923b","role":"QUAL","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1123,"prompt_tokens":18488,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":19611},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"0964874e62f9b1747994f8980624656b088b30d1c1ad40ad5d90431dc1e7923b","role":"QUAL","role_ordinal":0,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}